Section I: PREAMBLE

The Job Advertisements Bias Dataset (JABD) is a corpus of recruitment text in which
human annotators have marked the words and phrases that carry linguistic bias
relating to gender, ethnicity, age, religion and disability. It exists so that
researchers and practitioners can study, measure and reduce the ways recruitment
language discourages qualified people from applying.

That same property makes it dual-use. A resource that identifies which expressions
attract or deter which groups can, if inverted, be used to compose exclusionary
advertisements, or to search for wording that produces an exclusionary effect while
evading detection. The corpus is also an imperfect instrument: it records that a
protected attribute has been invoked, not that the invocation is hostile, and its
labels for implicit ("Coded") categories carry documented, measured disagreement
between annotators.

This License therefore strives for both open and responsible downstream use. For
the open character, we took inspiration from permissive open-source licenses in the
grant of intellectual property rights: access, reuse, modification and
redistribution are permitted free of charge, including for commercial purposes.
For the responsible character, we add use-based restrictions that do not permit
certain uses of the Data, so that the Licensor can act where misuse occurs.

The restrictions travel with the Data. Downstream derivative versions may be
released under different licensing terms, but those terms must always include - at
minimum - the same use-based restrictions as the ones in this License. They apply
to models trained on the Data exactly as they apply to the Data itself; training a
model does not launder the restrictions away.

